Hello alls, I'm doing unofficial research for my school's Academic
Computing Advisory Committe (read: they neither know, approve of, or
disapprove of my doing this :) regarding restricting access to
newsgroups because of content, namely situations where content may
be illegal (copyright violations, obsenity, etc...).

My gut (and discussions on the caf mailing list) tell me that if they
decide to censor a handful of newsgroups based on content, they
are opening themselves up to being responsable for _all_ content
that comes in over our nntp server.  What I'm looking for is real
information to back that up.  I've already culled a couple of articles
from the caf archive - but most of these seem to deal with situations
where sysops were exonerated from being responsible for the content of
what's on their boards.  What i'm looking for - is somewhere where a 
sysop or sysadmin, because they censored some info based on content - was
held responsible for _all_ the content passing through their system.
Either in a case which is used for precedent, or perhaps in a law or
school policy somewhere.

Thanks in advance for any info!

